TITLE:: ChaosOsc
categories:: UGens>Generators>Chaotic
summary:: Logistic-map chaotic oscillator with an interpolating iteration rate.
related:: Classes/LeakDC, Classes/K2A

DESCRIPTION::
ChaosOsc iterates the logistic map code::x = r * x * (1 - x):: and scales the
map state from the range 0 to 1 to an output in the range -1 to 1
(code::2 * x - 1::). The sequence is deterministic for a given seed and
chaosAmount, so renders are reproducible. It is provided by the ChaosOsc
server plugin.

The constructors are code::ChaosOsc.ar(chaosAmount, seed, freq, mul, add)::
and code::ChaosOsc.kr(chaosAmount, seed, freq, mul, add)::.

By default (code::freq = inf::) the map advances once per output sample:
once per audio sample for .ar and once per control block for .kr. This gives
broadband, noise-like output, and a two-argument call such as
code::ChaosOsc.ar(3.82, seed):: is bit-identical to the original two-argument
ChaosOsc. A lower freq advances the map freq times per second and linearly
interpolates between successive map states, turning the same chaotic sequence
into a smooth, wandering modulation source. A freq of 0 or below holds the
current value.

subsection:: Output range and DC offset
The output stays within -1 to 1 (before mul and add), but it does not fill
that range evenly and it is emphasis::not:: zero-mean. The largest value is
code::chaosAmount / 2 - 1:: (0.95 at 3.9) and the mean is positive: roughly
+0.19 at chaosAmount 3.9 and up to about +0.34 near 3.7. At the bottom of the
clamped range (3.57) the output only spans about -0.32 to 0.79. Before mixing
ChaosOsc as an audio signal, remove the offset with
code::LeakDC.ar(ChaosOsc.ar(...))::, as in the examples. The plugin keeps the
offset so that existing material renders unchanged.

Some chaosAmount values inside the clamped range fall in periodic windows of
the logistic map (for example a period-3 cycle around 3.83 to 3.84) and sound
pitched rather than noisy.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar, kr
Create an audio-rate or control-rate chaotic oscillator.

ARGUMENT:: chaosAmount
The logistic-map growth rate, clamped by the plugin to the range 3.57 to 3.999
(NaN uses 3.57). Defaults to 3.9. Audio-rate inputs are read per sample;
scalar and control-rate inputs are read once per block, so control-rate
changes take effect on the next block. A new chaosAmount only affects the
output on samples where the map advances.

ARGUMENT:: seed
The initial map state, normally in the open interval 0 to 1 (values at or
beyond 0 or 1 are nudged just inside; NaN uses 0.5). Defaults to 0.5. The seed
is captured when the Synth is created; changing the input afterward does not
reseed the oscillator.

ARGUMENT:: freq
Map iterations per second (Hz). Defaults to code::inf::. Read once per block,
whatever its input rate.
list::
## At or above the unit's sample rate (code::SampleRate.ir:: for .ar,
code::ControlRate.ir:: for .kr), including the default, the map advances once
per output sample.
## Between 0 and the sample rate, the map advances freq times per second and
the output is linearly interpolated between successive map states, so it moves
by at most code::2 * freq / sampleRate:: per output sample.
## At 0 or below, the oscillator holds (freezes) its current value. Raising
freq again continues from the held value.
## NaN behaves like the default.
::

ARGUMENT:: mul
Output will be multiplied by this value. Defaults to 1.0.

ARGUMENT:: add
This value will be added to the output. Defaults to 0.0.

RETURNS::
The ChaosOsc output with code::madd(mul, add):: applied. With the default mul
and add this is the ChaosOsc UGen itself, so no extra UGen is added.

EXAMPLES::

code::
// Audio-rate chaotic noise. LeakDC removes the positive DC offset.
{
    var noise = LeakDC.ar(ChaosOsc.ar(3.9, 0.37));
    (noise * 0.1) ! 2
}.play;
::

code::
// Slow, smoothly interpolated modulation: 4 map steps per second at control rate.
{
    var wander = ChaosOsc.kr(3.95, 0.21, 4);
    var pitch = wander.linexp(-1, 1, 200, 800);
    (SinOsc.ar(pitch) * 0.1) ! 2
}.play;
::

code::
// Audio-rate interpolation: lower freq values give a softer, smoother texture.
{
    var freq = MouseX.kr(100, SampleRate.ir, 1);
    (LeakDC.ar(ChaosOsc.ar(3.9, 0.37, freq)) * 0.1) ! 2
}.play;
::

code::
// Freeze and release: hold the filter sweep while the mouse button is down.
{
    var rate = 8 * (1 - MouseButton.kr(0, 1, 0));
    var cutoff = ChaosOsc.kr(3.9, 0.5, rate).linexp(-1, 1, 300, 3000);
    (LPF.ar(Saw.ar(110), cutoff) * 0.1) ! 2
}.play;
::
